  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   We use a multi-resolution optimization approach with curriculum learning to improve upon the parent solution. Our goal is to find a step function that minimizes the maximum overlap integral. We address the problem's non-convexity by combining global search (binary simulated annealing), local refinement (constrained optimization), and resolution escalation (curriculum learning). This multi-stage approach allows escaping shallow local optima while leveraging structure from higher-resolution parent solutions. We focus on convex relaxations and gradient-based optimization for continuous representations, preserving the parent's key features while refining the solution. The main bottleneck is the optimization landscape, so we prioritize exploratory moves in global phases and exploitative refinement in local phases.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n\n   - Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n, dx = 2/n, \u222bh dx = 1\n   - Objective: f(h) = max_k [ (1/n) * (1/n-1) * \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx ] approximately via discrete convolution\n   - Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i, \u2211h[i] = n/2\n   - Resolution representation: Start from parent resolution n0 (240 points), then 2\u00b7n0, 3\u00b7n0, 4\u00b7n0 (up to 960 points)\n\n   For each resolution s\u00b7n0:\n     \u2022 Use interpolated parent solution for initialization\n     \u2022 Perform binary representation search followed by continuous optimization\n     \u2022 Compute candidate fitness using exact objective\n\n   Formally, we optimize:\n     Minimize F(h) = max_{k=0}^{n-1} [ dx * (1/dx) * (1/(n-1)) * \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx ]\n   subject to:\n     \u222b_{0}^2 h(x) dx = 1\n     0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n\n   We use CVXPY for convex subproblems and SLSQP for local optimization. The smooth_max_and_grad function provides gradient estimates for the non-convex objective.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   For a given resolution n = s\u00b7n0:\n     - Compute dx = 2/n\n     - Initialize h from interpolated parent solution\n     - Project to satisfy sum constraint\n     - Phase 1: Binary representation search\n        * Use temperature-based simulated annealing\n        * Perturb via bit-vector flipping (neighbor generation)\n        * Accept based on objective improvement and Metropolis criterion\n        * Maintain separate temperature schedule and cooling rate\n     - Phase 2: Continuous refinement\n        * Project best vector to continuous space\n        * Use projected gradient descent with box constraints\n        * Apply SLSQP with gradient constraint\n        * Use smooth_max_and_grad for objective approximation\n        * Track constraints via clipping and projecting\n     - Periodically compare solutions and update best overall\n     - Escalate resolution if time allows and solution improves\n\n   Key operations:\n     def compute_c5(h):\n        corr = np.correlate(h,1-h,mode=\"full\")\n        return np.max(corr)*dx\n\n     def gradient_estimate(h,alpha):\n        corr = np.correlate(h,1-h,mode=\"full\")\n        return smooth_max_and_grad(corr,alpha)\n\n     def project_array_sum(h, target_sum):\n        diff = target_sum - np.sum(h)\n        # Distribute change proportionally and apply clipping\n        h = np.clip(h.astype(float) + diff/len(h), 0, 1)\n        return h\n\n   Stops conditions:\n     * Time budget exhaustion\n     * Resolution escalation limit (max n=960)\n     * Consecutive improvement limits\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Binary search: Temperature starts at 0.1, cools by factor 0.999997\n   - Continuous optimization: Gradient descent with learning rate 0.01\n   - Resolution escalation: Double resolution (2,4,8 times parent)\n   * Data perturbation: Gaussian noise standard deviation 0.2\n   * Diversification formula: New vector = project(parent, new_resolution, \u03b1=0.0)\n\n5. Budget and robustness:\n   - Estimated time per resolution: 600s for n=960\n   - Check budget before each resolution phase\n   - Maintain best solution from all resolutions\n   - Regularly save elite candidates to disk (simulation)\n   - Use convex penalties if improving fails\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Final candidate must satisfy: sum(h)=n/2, min(h)>0.9 or max(h)<0.1\n   - Evaluate exact C5 via: compute_c5(h)\n   - Compare against current best (0.3808)\n   - Return tuple (h_values, C5, n)\n   - All intermediate candidates must be within [0,1]",
